  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday through next Wednesday)\nIssued at 1235 PM MST Thu Nov 12 2026\n\nSystem #2, a weakly closed low, will be on the heels of the first\nsystem, but is expected to cross near the NM/CO border Saturday\nand Saturday night. Ascent ahead of this system will allow rain\nshowers to develop across northwest NM as early as sunrise\nSaturday. Showers will then expand across much of northern NM\nthrough the day, reaching at least as far south as the I-40 \ncorridor. Enough instability exists for a few thunderstorms, \nespecially along and east of the Sangre de Cristo Mtns. There \nremains potential for additional flooding across northwest NM \ngiven how saturated soils remain, though chances remain low. \nRegardless, any additional precipitation will not help the muddy \nroads, recovery efforts, etc. across the area. Snow levels will \nbe near 9000 feet. Above this level, between 1 and 6 inches of \nthe white stuff is expected, with the west slopes above 9kft \nfavored. A Winter Weather Advisory or two may be needed if trends \ncontinue. An associated weak Pacific cold front will also race \nfrom west to east on Saturday. Breezy to locally windy westerly \nwinds can be expected behind it. The low will shift into the \nPanhandles shortly after midnight Saturday night, and \nprecipitation will quickly taper off. One exception will be light\nwrap-around precip across northeast NM that will linger for a few\nmore hours. \n\nLow temperatures will be chilly Saturday night after the precip\nends and the clouds clear. Places like Farmington, ABQ Valley, \nMoriarty and Santa Fe are expected to freeze. \n\nQuiet weather returns Sunday and Monday as upper level ridging\nmoves into the area. A backdoor front will slide down the plains\non Sunday, but high temps will only be a few degrees cooler than\non Saturday.\n\nThe next storm system is expected to impact NM starting Tuesday. A\nsharp trough across the western U.S. will slide eastward before\nlifting northeastward Tuesday over the Four Corners. Models\nsuggest there is no subtropical tap of moisture ahead of this\nsystem, so we'll only be working with the moisture it brings with\nit. It appears northwest NM will be favored for precipitation \nwith orographic lift as the primary forcing. It should be noted\nwhile most operational models show this solution, ensembles\nsuggest at least light precipitation will be possible just about\nanywhere.  \n\nWednesday looks to be quieter but another, potentially stronger,\nsystem may be in the offing late next week.",
